{
  "fromId": "k",
  "toId": "x",
  "preamble": "The lenition or spirantization of a voiceless velar stop /k/ into a velar fricative /x/, a classic example of weakening in the consonant system. Cuzco Quechua and Imbabura Kichwa spirantize coda *k to [x] (*suk > Cuzco hux, Imbabura ʃux 'one').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Spirantization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Biblical Hebrew",
      "languageFamily": "Semitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "melek [k] (king)",
          "to": "מֶלֶךְ [mɛlɛx]",
          "note": "Begedkefat spirantization: post-vocalic /k/ → [x] in Biblical Hebrew. Final kaf is always [x]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "High German",
      "languageFamily": "Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "book /k/",
          "to": "Buch /x/",
          "note": "High German consonant shift of final /k/ to /x/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Greek (Ancient)",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/k/",
          "to": "/x/",
          "note": "Spirantization of aspirated stops."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Iranian",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *k before consonant (e.g. *kš, *km, *kr)",
          "to": "Proto-Iranian *x (e.g. *xš, *xm, *xr)",
          "note": "Partial Grimm's Law in Iranian: voiceless velar /k/ became velar fricative /x/ before another consonant. E.g., Proto-Indo-Iranian *kšap- 'night' → Avestan xšap-, Old Persian xšāyaθiya 'king'. The cluster *xš is preserved in Ossetian ӕхсин (æxsin) 'lady'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Kurdish",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Iranian *kt, *kš clusters",
          "to": "Kurdish /xt/, /xš/",
          "note": "Kurdish, like other Iranian languages, preserves the Proto-Iranian k→x shift in consonant clusters. E.g., Kurdish xwendin 'to read' shows the fricative reflex of inherited *k in cluster position."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Buryat",
      "languageFamily": "Mongolic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Mongolic *k (strong velar stop, non-initial)",
          "to": "Buryat /x/ [x]",
          "note": "One of the most salient diachronic features of Buryat is the spirantization of the inherited strong velar stop *k to the voiceless velar fricative /x/. This parallels the affricate spirantizations *c→s and *j→z in Buryat and represents a general weakening tendency in the consonant system. E.g., Proto-Mongolic *kele 'tongue' → Buryat xele."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Nuer (Western dialects)",
      "languageFamily": "Nilo-Saharan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "[k] (morpheme-final velar stop)",
          "to": "[ɣ] ~ [x]",
          "note": "Western Nuer: morpheme-final /k/ spirantizes to velar fricative [ɣ] (voiced) in intervocalic position, with possible devoicing to [x] in phrase-final position. This consonantal lenition operates as a morphophonological function in Western Nuer, affecting morpheme-final velars. The Jikany (Eastern) dialect preserves [k] in these same contexts, despite the same orthographic representation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Kabyle",
      "languageFamily": "Berber",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/k/ (lax velar stop)",
          "to": "[x] or [ç]",
          "note": "Kabyle spirantization: lax /k/ in post-vocalic and intervocalic positions weakens to the velar or palatal fricative [x]/[ç]. As in all Berber spirantization, geminate /kk/ resists the shift. Kabyle is classified as a spirantizing Berber language alongside other northern varieties."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Middle Atlas Tamazight",
      "languageFamily": "Berber",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/k/ in weak prosodic position",
          "to": "[x] or [ç]",
          "note": "Middle Atlas Tamazight (Ayt Ndhir dialect area) spirantizes lax /k/ to [x] or the palatal variant [ç]. The process is conditioned by syllable position and tense/lax phonation contrast; found throughout central and northern Moroccan Berber dialects."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
